Explain the origin, function, and characteristics of the hanger steak in relation to cattle anatomy
The hanger steak originates from the diaphragm of a steer or heifer, specifically from the crura, or legs, of the diaphragm. It is anatomically positioned to hang from the diaphragm and is attached to the last rib and the front of several lumbar vertebrae. Functionally, the diaphragm plays a crucial role in respiration, and the hanger steak, as part of this muscle, would have been involved in supporting this function.

In terms of characteristics, the hanger steak is noted for being tender and flavorful, often described as the tenderest cut on an animal. It resembles flank steak in texture and flavor but is distinct due to its V-shaped form and the presence of an inedible membrane running down the middle. The cut is typically found on the plate, which is the lower belly of the animal. Its unique position and structure contribute to its distinct qualities and appeal among beef enthusiasts.
