Identify the key historical context and legislative background of the Pure Food and Drug Act, including its primary objectives and the era in which it was enacted.
The Pure Food and Drug Act was enacted during the Progressive Era in 1906. Its primary objectives were to ban foreign and interstate traffic in adulterated or mislabeled food and drug products, ensure accurate labeling of drugs by requiring active ingredients to be listed on packaging, and establish purity standards based on the United States Pharmacopeia. The Act led to the creation of the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and was a significant step in consumer protection legislation. It was also known as the "Wiley Act" after its chief advocate, Harvey Wiley. The legislation was prompted by concerns over unsafe and misleading products, such as the contaminated diphtheria antitoxin that resulted in the deaths of thirteen children in St. Louis, Missouri.
