Analyze the legal and tax classification of Jaffa cakes to determine whether they are categorized as a biscuit or a cake under relevant regulatory frameworks
The legal and tax classification of Jaffa cakes has been determined through a VAT tribunal in 1991. The court ruled in favor of McVitie's, classifying Jaffa cakes as cakes for tax purposes. This classification means that value added tax (VAT) is not payable on Jaffa cakes in the United Kingdom. In Ireland, they are also regarded as cakes by Revenue due to their moisture content exceeding 12%. Despite their size and shape, which might suggest a biscuit, the court emphasized that the product was considered a cake based on its composition and intended use. Therefore, under relevant regulatory frameworks, Jaffa cakes are categorized as cakes.
