The song "Streets of Laredo" originates from the Irish folk song "The Unfortunate Rake." It was adapted into an American cowboy ballad and is also known as "The Cowboy's Lament." The adaptation is attributed to Frank H. Maynard, an old-time cowboy, and it has since become a folk music standard. The song has been performed, recorded, and adapted numerous times with many variations. 

For "Here Comes Santa Claus," the original version was written and performed by Gene Autry, with music composed by Oakley Haldeman. The song was inspired by the crowds chanting "Here comes Santa Claus" during the 1946 Santa Claus Lane Parade in Los Angeles.

Regarding "Steal Away," it is an American Negro spiritual, with variations including "Steal Away to Jesus," "Swing Low, Sweet Chariot," "Wade in the Water," and "Gospel Train." The song was composed by Wallace Willis, a Choctaw freedman in the old Indian Territory, sometime before 1862.
