Explain the constitutional significance and legal framework of the Charter within Canada's constitutional structure
The Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms is a fundamental part of Canada's constitutional structure, forming the first part of the Constitution Act, 1982. It serves as a bill of rights, guaranteeing political rights to Canadian citizens and civil rights to everyone in Canada. These rights are protected from the policies and actions of all levels of government, ensuring that no law or government action can infringe upon them without proper legal justification. The Charter aims to unify Canadians around a set of principles that embody these rights, promoting a shared understanding of individual freedoms and democratic values. Additionally, it is written in plain language to ensure accessibility and clarity for the average citizen. The Charter also complements Part I of the Constitution Act, 1982, which finalized Canada's legislative independence by ensuring that no further British Acts of Parliament will apply to Canada as part of its law.
