The longest canal tunnel is the Sapperton Canal Tunnel. It is located on the Thames and Severn Canal near Cirencester in Gloucestershire, England. The tunnel has a length of 3,817 yards (approximately 3,488 meters) and was the longest canal tunnel, and the longest tunnel of any kind in England from 1789 to 1811. The Thames and Severn Canal was authorized by an Act of Parliament on 17 April 1783, but the details of the tunnel were not finalized until after some debate regarding its size. The tunnel's construction was significant as it facilitated the connection between the Severn and Thames waterways, enabling the transportation of goods and contributing to the development of the region's infrastructure.
