The specific city in the United States that hosted the 1996 Summer Olympics was Atlanta, Georgia. The Games, officially known as the Games of the XXVI Olympiad, were held from July 19 to August 4, 1996. They marked the centenary of the 1896 Summer Olympics in Athens and were the fourth Summer Olympics to be hosted by the United States. The 1996 Atlanta Games were notable for being financially viable and having a positive economic impact on the city. Additionally, they were the first Summer Olympics since 1924 to be held in a different year from a Winter Olympics. However, the Games were also marred by a tragic event when a bomb exploded at Centennial Olympic Park on July 27, 1996, killing two people and injuring over 100.
