The anatomical regions of the brain where neurons are primarily located include the cerebral cortex, which is the outer layer of the brain and contains a vast number of neurons. Underneath the cerebral cortex are several important structures such as the thalamus, hypothalamus, pituitary gland, and basal ganglia, which also contain neurons. The limbic structures, including the amygdala and hippocampus, are also rich in neurons. Additionally, the basal forebrain and circumventricular organs contain neurons. Neurons are distributed across these structures, with the cerebral cortex containing more than 86 billion neurons, while other regions have varying numbers of neurons depending on their specific functions.
