Identify the anatomical location of the tenderloin within a cow's body and describe its position relative to other major cuts of beef.
The tenderloin is located in the loin section of a cow's body, specifically ventral to the transverse processes of the lumbar vertebrae, near the kidneys. It sits beneath the ribs, next to the backbone. The tenderloin spans two primal cuts: the short loin (called the "sirloin" in Commonwealth countries) and the sirloin (called the rump in Commonwealth countries). It is an oblong shape and is part of the psoas major muscle. Relative to other major cuts, the tenderloin is positioned along both sides of the spine and is distinct from the sirloin and rump areas.
