Identify the specific prehistoric period associated with the development and use of early human tools, including its defining characteristics and chronological placement in human history.
The specific prehistoric period associated with the development and use of early human tools is the Paleolithic. It is characterized by the original development of stone tools, which covers approximately 99% of human technological prehistory. The Paleolithic period extends from the earliest known use of stone tools by hominins around 3.3 million years ago to the end of the Pleistocene around 11,650 cal BP. During this time, hominins lived in small societies such as bands and subsisted by gathering plants, fishing, hunting, or scavenging wild animals. The Paleolithic is followed in Europe by the Mesolithic, although the transition date varies geographically.
