Identify the origin and founding details of the Boy Scouts of America, including its founder, year of establishment, and initial purpose.
The Boy Scouts of America (BSA) was founded in 1910 and was inspired by the Boy Scout Association established by Sir Robert Baden-Powell in Britain in 1908. The BSA was incorporated on February 8, 1910, following the efforts of Chicago publisher W. D. Boyce, who secured the rights to the Scouting program in the United States. The initial purpose of the BSA was to provide young boys with outdoor activities aimed at developing character, citizenship, and personal fitness qualities.
