Identify the historical context and legislative process behind the addition of 'under God' to the Pledge of Allegiance
The addition of the phrase "under God" to the Pledge of Allegiance was a result of a campaign led by the Knights of Columbus, a Catholic fraternal organization. In 1951, the Knights of Columbus began including the words "under God" in their version of the Pledge of Allegiance. This idea gained traction and spread throughout the organization nationwide over the next two years. 

In 1954, following pressure from the Knights of Columbus and other groups, President Dwight D. Eisenhower signed legislation that officially added the phrase "under God" to the Pledge of Allegiance. The legislative action was part of a Joint Resolution of Congress amending § 4 of the Flag Code enacted in 1942. The resolution was introduced by Rep. Charles Oakman (R-Mich.) on February 8, 1954, and signed into law on June 14, 1954, which is now celebrated as Flag Day.
