Identify the biological process involved in the movement of food through the esophagus and explain its mechanism and terminology
The biological process involved in the movement of food through the esophagus is **peristalsis**. Peristalsis is a series of rhythmic, unidirectional contractions of smooth muscles that propel food downward through the digestive tract. In the context of the esophagus, these contractions work to rapidly force the food into the stomach. The process is initiated after food is swallowed and moved from the mouth to the pharynx, then into the esophagus. Smooth muscles contract behind the bolus (chewed food) to prevent it from being squeezed back into the mouth, and rhythmic waves of contractions move the bolus toward the stomach. This mechanism is also aided by the migrating motor complex (MMC), which helps trigger peristaltic waves. Importantly, peristalsis in the esophagus operates in one direction only and does not reverse, even during vomiting, where food is expelled through contraction of abdominal muscles.
