Identify the historical dispute between Virginia and Maryland and determine the key issues that led to the conflict.
The historical dispute between Virginia and Maryland centered on their conflicting land boundaries along the Potomac River. The key issues that led to the conflict were the differing interpretations of the river's boundary as defined by land grants from King James II. Maryland's grant, made to Cecilius Calvert, 2nd Baron Baltimore, established the boundary at the low-water mark of the southern bank of the Potomac River. In contrast, Virginia's grant, given to Thomas Colepeper, 2nd Baron Colepeper, designated "the Potomac River" as the boundary. This discrepancy resulted in a long-running border dispute between the two colonies, which persisted even after they became states. The unresolved boundary issue contributed to ongoing conflicts and disputes over land ownership and jurisdiction.
