Identify the historical origin and key legislative milestones of the Free School Lunch Program, including its inception date and initial implementation.
The Free School Lunch Program, also known as the National School Lunch Program (NSLP), originated with the Richard B. Russell National School Lunch Act, signed into law by President Harry S. Truman in 1946. This act was designed to provide low-cost or free school lunches to qualified students, while also addressing agricultural surpluses by increasing food consumption. The program's initial implementation focused on ensuring that meals met minimum nutrition requirements and provided access to meals for students in participating schools. Key legislative milestones include the establishment of the NSLP under the 1946 act, which laid the foundation for the program's structure and objectives.
