Determine the specific year and context in which the program was officially launched, along with any significant events or policies that contributed to its establishment.
The National School Lunch Program (NSLP) was officially launched in 1946. It was established through the National School Lunch Act, which was signed into law by President Harry S. Truman. The program was influenced by concerns over child nutrition, particularly after an investigation revealed that poor health among men rejected for World War II service was linked to childhood malnutrition. Additionally, the program was shaped by agricultural and food interests, as well as the agenda of the United States Department of Agriculture (USDA). The NSLP was framed as a measure of national security to safeguard children's health and encourage domestic consumption of nutritious agricultural commodities.
