Explain the purpose of a colonoscopy and the role of the tube (colonoscope) in the procedure
The purpose of a colonoscopy is to examine the large bowel (colon) and the distal part of the small bowel using a flexible tube called a colonoscope. The colonoscope, equipped with a CCD camera or fiber optic camera, allows for visual inspection of the intestinal lining. It enables the detection of abnormalities such as ulceration, polyps, or lesions, and facilitates procedures like biopsy or removal of suspicious colorectal cancer lesions. The tube plays a crucial role in providing a clear view of the gastrointestinal tract and enabling therapeutic interventions during the examination.
