Identify the historical context and key events that led to the division of the Dutch colony of New Netherlands
The division of the Dutch colony of New Netherland was primarily a result of the Anglo-Dutch Wars, particularly the Second Anglo-Dutch War (1665–1667). This conflict arose due to tensions between England and the Dutch Republic, including disputes over trade and colonial territories. A significant factor was the use of propaganda, such as the Amboyna Massacre, which was referenced to justify English actions against Dutch colonies. The Treaty of Breda in 1667 ended the Second Anglo-Dutch War and resulted in the transfer of New Netherland to English control. Following this, the British split the colony into two parts, naming them New York and New Jersey. Additionally, the village of Bergen, which had been part of New Netherland, was required to garrison English soldiers after its capture, further contributing to the colony's fragmentation under English rule.
