Describe the technical process and global implementation of this practice, including time zones and daylight saving adjustments
The technical process of daylight saving time (DST) involves advancing clocks by one hour during the summer months to extend evening daylight, while sacrificing earlier sunrise times. This adjustment is typically made close to the start of spring by moving clocks forward one hour and reverting them back in autumn by moving clocks backward one hour. 

Time zones play a crucial role in implementing DST, as regions within the same time zone generally adopt the same rules. However, some countries or regions may have unique adjustments. The combination of time zones and DST rules, along with synchronized timekeeping services, determines the legal civil time for any location.

Globally, the implementation of DST varies. Some countries observe it, while others do not. Countries that do observe DST typically adjust their clocks in accordance with local regulations, which may differ in terms of start and end dates. Additionally, some higher latitude and temperate zone countries use DST for part of the year, creating a permanent daylight saving time effect due to skewed land time zones relative to nautical time zones.
