Identify the historical context and key events related to census-taking in British India to determine the earliest recorded census
The earliest recorded census in British India is often considered the 1865 census of the North-West Provinces, which is sometimes referred to as the first proper census in India. Prior to this, there were quantitative exercises conducted by the East India Company, but these were described as fragmentary, lacking in uniformity, and not systematic. The British Raj authorities concluded the first "all-India census" in 1872, although it did not cover all regions and was conducted between 1867 and 1872. The first synchronous decennial census was conducted in 1881.
