Determine the exact year and administrative authority responsible for the first census in India, including any relevant colonial records or official documentation
The first census in India was conducted in the year 1872. The administrative authority responsible for this census was the British Raj, which operated under the auspices of the British Crown following the Indian Rebellion of 1857. This census marked the beginning of systematic enumeration across the country, although it did not cover all regions uniformly and was conducted asynchronously between 1867 and 1872. The British East India Company had previously carried out limited quantitative exercises, but these were considered fragmentary and lacked uniformity. The 1872 census is often regarded as the first proper census in India, setting the foundation for future decennial censuses, with the first synchronous decennial census taking place in 1881.
