Identify the historical context and operational role of the Memphis Belle during World War II, including its squadron, mission objectives, and significance in the war effort.
The Memphis Belle was a Boeing B-17 Flying Fortress of the US Army Air Force, specifically assigned to the 401st Bomb Squadron. During World War II, it participated in several bombing missions over Nazi-occupied Europe, with its most notable missions occurring in April 1943 during the Bremen campaign and later in May 1943 when it completed its 25th mission to Lorient, France. The aircraft's participation in these missions was significant as it became one of the first B-17s to complete 25 missions, which was a major achievement for the Eighth Air Force. The Memphis Belle's crew, under the command of Captain Morgan, was selected to complete their tour together and return to the United States for a war bond drive, highlighting the aircraft's symbolic importance in boosting morale and support for the war effort.
