Determine the specific years and phases of construction for the U.S. Capitol building and other significant structures in Washington, D.C., and how they relate to the establishment of the capital.
The construction of the U.S. Capitol building began in 1793, with the original building completed in 1800. The Capitol was designed by Pierre Charles L'Enfant, who also planned the city of Washington, D.C., specifying the location for the "Congress House" (the Capitol) and the "President's House" (later known as the White House). The original building was later expanded, particularly with the addition of the massive dome and enlarged chambers for Congress.

The Capitol Reflecting Pool, part of the United States Capitol Complex, was constructed as part of the expansion and development of the Capitol grounds, though the exact year of its construction is not specified in the provided context. The establishment of Washington, D.C., as the capital of the United States was closely tied to these constructions, with the Capitol serving as the seat of the legislative branch and a central symbol of the federal government.
