Identify the historical context and key figures involved in the abolition of sati in India, including the relevant colonial and indigenous rulers.
The historical context of the abolition of sati in India involved significant efforts by both colonial and indigenous rulers, as well as reformers. Sati, the practice of a widow burning herself alive on her husband's funeral pyre, was abolished in British India in 1829 by Governor-General Lord William Bentinck, following the advocacy of Raja Rammohan Roy. Roy, a prominent social reformer, began campaigning against the practice after witnessing his sister-in-law's sati. His efforts led to the passage of the Bengal Sati Regulation in 1829, which officially banned the practice.

Additionally, various princely states, including Baroda, Kolhapur, Indore, Satara, Nagpur, Mysore, Jaipur, Hyderabad, Gwalior, Jammu and Kashmir, Awadh, Bhopal, and Cutch, also banned sati at different times between 1840 and 1852. These actions were part of a broader movement that included indigenous rulers who sought to modernize and align with British policies while addressing local customs and traditions. The abolition of sati marked a significant step in the social reform movements in India during the colonial period.
