Determine the significance and symbolism of the Olympic flame in ancient and modern contexts
The Olympic flame holds significant symbolic meaning in both ancient and modern contexts. In ancient Greece, the flame represented divine connotations, as it was believed to have been stolen from the gods by Prometheus. It was present at many sanctuaries, including Olympia, where a sacred fire burned on the altar of Hestia. During the Olympic Games, which honored Zeus, additional fires were lit at his temple and that of his wife, Hera.

In the modern context, the Olympic flame symbolizes the continuity of the Olympic movement. It was reintroduced at the 1928 Summer Olympics in Amsterdam and has since become a central element of the Games. The flame is lit at Olympia, Greece, months before the Games, and is carried through a torch relay to the opening ceremony, where it is used to light the Olympic cauldron. The flame continues to burn throughout the Games and is extinguished during the closing ceremony, representing the enduring spirit and unity of the Olympic ideals.
