Identify the anatomical structure of a plant leaf and locate the mesophyll within it, including its position relative to other tissues such as epidermis and vascular bundles.
The anatomical structure of a plant leaf includes the epidermis, mesophyll, and vascular bundles. The mesophyll is located between the upper and lower epidermal layers. It consists of two main regions: the palisade mesophyll, which is positioned on the upper side of the leaf blade (adaxial surface), and the spongy mesophyll, which is found beneath the palisade mesophyll, closer to the lower epidermis (abaxial surface). The vascular bundles are situated within the mesophyll, with the xylem oriented toward the adaxial surface and the phloem toward the abaxial surface.
