Analyze the sequence of events leading up to the revolt, including the catalysts and the first significant act of rebellion that is widely recognized as the starting point of the 1857 revolt.
The sequence of events leading up to the Indian Rebellion of 1857 was marked by growing discontent among various groups in India, including soldiers (sepoys), peasants, and local rulers, against British rule. Several factors contributed to this unrest, such as economic exploitation, cultural insensitivity, and religious tensions. One significant catalyst was the introduction of the Enfield rifle, which required greased cartridges that were rumored to be made from cow and pig fat, offending both Hindu and Muslim soldiers.

The first significant act of rebellion widely recognized as the starting point of the 1857 revolt occurred on 29 March 1857, when Mangal Pandey, a sepoy stationed at the Meerut cantonment, rebelled against his British officers. This act of defiance is often considered the spark that ignited the larger uprising. Following this, the rebellion spread across northern and central India, eventually leading to the broader Indian Rebellion of 1857.
