Detail the procedural requirements and thresholds for filing a case in the New York City Civil Court, including monetary limits and subject-matter jurisdiction.
The New York City Civil Court handles civil cases involving claims for damages up to $25,000. It includes a small claims part for cases involving amounts up to $5,000 and a housing part for landlord-tenant matters. The court also has equity jurisdiction limited to real property actions, ejectment actions, and actions involving the foreclosure of real property within the monetary limit. The court's subject-matter jurisdiction includes replevin when the value of the chattel does not exceed $25,000, real property actions such as partitions, and foreclosures within the monetary limit. The court is a single citywide court and is part of the New York State Unified Court System.
