Identify the historical context and canonical development of the New Testament to determine when the book of James was considered authoritative and included in the biblical canon.
The book of James was included among the twenty-seven New Testament books first listed by Athanasius of Alexandria in his "Thirty-Ninth Festal Epistle" (AD 367). It was confirmed as a canonical epistle of the New Testament by a series of councils in the Fourth Century. While its authenticity was doubted by some, including Theodore, Bishop of Mopsuestia in Cilicia, it was ultimately accepted as part of the canon. The process of canonization of the New Testament was complex and lengthy, with the recognition of these writings as authoritative formalized in various councils, such as the Second Council of Trullan in 692 for the Orthodox and the Catholic Church's dogmatic definition in 382.
