Provide a concise overview of the structure and jurisdiction of Allahabad High Court to contextualize the role of the chief judge.
The Allahabad High Court is one of the oldest high courts in India, established on 17 March 1866 as the High Court of Judicature for the North-Western Provinces at Agra. It was later relocated to Allahabad in 1869 and renamed the High Court of Judicature at Allahabad in 1919. The court serves the state of Uttar Pradesh and has a permanent circuit bench in Lucknow, which functions as the administrative capital of the state. 

The court has a sanctioned strength of 160 judges, including 76 permanent and 84 additional judges. As of 22 November 2018, there were 109 serving judges. The Chief Justice of the court, currently Justice Govind Mathur, plays a pivotal role in overseeing the administration and judicial proceedings of the court. The structure and jurisdiction of the Allahabad High Court are integral to understanding the responsibilities and significance of its chief judge within the Indian legal system.
