The National Institutes of Health (NIH) is the primary federal agency in the United States responsible for biomedical and public health research. It was established in the late 1870s and is part of the Department of Health and Human Services. The NIH's main responsibilities include conducting its own scientific research through the Intramural Research Program (IRP) and providing funding for biomedical research at non-NIH facilities through the Extramural Research Program. Additionally, the NIH comprises 27 separate institutes and centers that focus on various aspects of biomedical science.
